# Tool Input Validation

| Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| Issue | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
| Status | Draft |

The registry enforces the schema advertised to providers before invoking a tool executor, so malformed calls can be corrected within the existing conversation.

## Contract

1. "'`execute` rejects missing required fields with an explanatory `ToolResponse` and no executor invocation, passes accepted input and unknown properties unchanged exactly once without coercion or defaults, and preserves the unknown-tool exception.
2. Wrong types and enum violations return property paths and failed constraints without executing the tool or exposing input values in results or logs.
3. Static schemas compile at registration, invalid schemas throw an error naming the tool at registration, and ten executions reuse the compiled schema without inspecting it again.
4. Function-valued definitions validate the schema resolved for the current call, compile newly resolved schema objects, and reuse compilation when the same schema object is returned again.

## Integration and delivery

Rejections are tool results visible to the model on the next round; the existing five-tool-round budget bounds correction cycles, and an unknown tool name continues to throw `Unknown tool: <name>`.

Use a runtime JSON Schema validator dependency, with no opt-out, type coercion, default insertion or implicit ban on additional properties; the one-line `execute` doc comment points here for the full contract.

Update README.md, docs/adding-a-tool.md and .specify/spec.md to describe the enforced input contract and retry behavior; document the executor migration, publish a MINOR version bump, and add an npm-reader-facing CHANGELOG.md entry.
